<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Zamfara</h1><p class="st-meta">1200 CE – 1808 CE (approx.) · capital Birnin Zamfara</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Hausa kingdom of the north-western plains that rose to power in the 16th and 17th centuries before being destroyed by its rival Gobir.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Zamfara was counted among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banza-bakwai">Banza Bakwai</a>, the 'illegitimate' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa states</a> outside the core seven. Its people occupied the fertile valley of the Zamfara river west of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/katsina-kingdom">Katsina</a>. Tradition dates its first rulers to around 1200, though the kingdom becomes visible in records only in the 16th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>From its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birnin-zamfara">Birnin Zamfara</a> the kingdom expanded in the 17th century, raiding as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> and controlling routes between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hausa-kingdoms">Hausaland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>. Its rulers combined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> royal rites, and the state's power rested on cavalry and slave labour on its farms.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1762 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-gobir">Gobir</a> under Babari sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birnin-zamfara">Birnin Zamfara</a> and occupied much of its land. The surviving Zamfara state, based at Anka, was conquered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulani-war">Fulani jihad</a> around 1808 and came under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>. Its name lives on in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamfara-state">Zamfara State</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-zamfara.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Zamfara" width="320" height="308"><figcaption>Image: Henry B. Lovejoy · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_western_Hausaland_in_the_late_18th-century.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1200 CE – 1808 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Birnin Zamfara</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam and Hausa traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hausa</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zamfara</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1504&amp;focus=kingdom-of-zamfara" aria-label="Kingdom of Zamfara on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4876 1823 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Zamfara lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5172 1927h0M5144 1947h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5196 2079h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5196 2079h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Nigeria. <a href="/atlas?year=1504&amp;focus=kingdom-of-zamfara">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

